WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:34.099
Hello, I am Peter Walter.  I work at the University of California at San Francisco,

00:00:34.100 --> 00:00:39.232
and we are basically cell biologists trying to figure out how cells work,

00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:43.699
specializing on organelle biogenesis and how proteins

00:00:43.700 --> 00:00:45.699
get to the right place and how they function.

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:49.499
And ultimately we would like to understand how proteins work as molecular machines

00:00:49.500 --> 00:00:53.666
to make cells as wonderful and as complicated as they are.

00:00:53.667 --> 00:00:57.666
So what I want to tell you today is a brief story of discovery

00:00:57.667 --> 00:01:03.532
I think one of the most wonderful discoveries that was made in my lab in my career,

00:01:03.533 --> 00:01:08.099
and it involves the reaction by which cells

00:01:08.100 --> 00:01:11.766
decide how much of a particular organelle they have to have.

00:01:11.767 --> 00:01:16.499
And the organelle that I'm going to tell you about is the endoplasmic reticulum,

00:01:16.500 --> 00:01:20.132
which is the first way station which proteins enter

00:01:20.133 --> 00:01:26.166
as they move through the secretory pathway to the surface of the cell,

00:01:26.167 --> 00:01:29.599
and end up being secreted or inserted in the plasma membrane.

00:01:29.600 --> 00:01:35.699
The endoplasmic reticulum then is a place where proteins have to...

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:38.599
They enter the ER; they have to fold;

00:01:38.600 --> 00:01:42.899
they have to become assembled from multiple subunits; they become modified,

00:01:42.900 --> 00:01:47.066
maybe a carbohydrate is added; disulfide bonds are formed.

00:01:47.067 --> 00:01:51.432
And all of these reactions are important to produce properly functioning proteins.

00:01:51.433 --> 00:01:55.499
And cells that are specialized, that make a lot of proteins and secrete them,

00:01:55.500 --> 00:01:57.499
have to have a lot of endoplasmic reticulum

00:01:57.500 --> 00:02:06.166
in order to carry out these processes with fidelity and with appropriately abundant machinery.

00:02:06.167 --> 00:02:08.932
The proteins enter the endoplasmic reticulum

00:02:08.933 --> 00:02:11.699
in an unfolded state as they come out of the ribosome

00:02:11.700 --> 00:02:15.999
and then as they are in this organelle they basically have to mature.

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.832
And if they cannot fold properly,

00:02:17.833 --> 00:02:25.599
then a cell basically puts proteins in its plasma membrane or secretes them

00:02:25.600 --> 00:02:33.166
and because all the machinery that is required for a cell to know where it is in the body,

00:02:33.167 --> 00:02:37.832
what it has to do, how it has to behave in the context of a multicellular organism,

00:02:37.833 --> 00:02:42.899
all this information is being transmitted by proteins that are being secreted

00:02:42.900 --> 00:02:45.966
or by the machineries that sit in the plasma membrane.

00:02:45.967 --> 00:02:51.032
So it's very, very important that these machineries work properly, are properly assembled

00:02:51.033 --> 00:02:55.866
because otherwise you may create a rogue cell that doesn't know when to divide,

00:02:55.867 --> 00:03:02.066
when to differentiate, when to die, when to migrate to another place and so on and so forth.

00:03:02.067 --> 00:03:06.199
So, for the organism, this is a very important process.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.599
And this is sort of amplified in this slide

00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:15.299
where you look at the differentiation of this cell, a precursor cell here

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:19.732
that turns into a professional secretory cell, in this case it's a plasma cell.

00:03:19.733 --> 00:03:23.999
And the plasma cells are the cells that make antibodies that are being secreted in the bloodstream,

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:27.699
so this cell makes its own weight every day in antibody molecules.

00:03:27.700 --> 00:03:30.899
And you see that this differentiation event goes hand in hand

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.032
with this vast proliferation of endoplasmic reticulum

00:03:34.033 --> 00:03:36.799
that basically fills the cytosol wall to wall.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:41.299
So, how does that cell know how much endoplasmic reticulum it needs?

00:03:41.300 --> 00:03:46.699
So there must be signaling pathways in this cell that figure out how much of an organelle

00:03:46.700 --> 00:03:55.366
we have and how much should be there according to the needs of the secretory load that the cell has.

00:03:55.367 --> 00:03:59.932
And this signaling pathway that transmits that information,

00:03:59.933 --> 00:04:03.966
which we've been studying for the last fifteen years or so,

00:04:03.967 --> 00:04:06.799
is called the Unfolded Protein Response.

00:04:06.800 --> 00:04:12.332
And it's called the Unfolded Protein Response because it initiates with an accumulation of

00:04:12.333 --> 00:04:16.099
unfolded or misfolded proteins in the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:04:16.100 --> 00:04:20.866
And these proteins, they are unfolded because there isn't enough capacity

00:04:20.867 --> 00:04:23.232
to fold them properly from the machinery.

00:04:23.233 --> 00:04:25.966
These proteins then create a signal that's being transmitted

00:04:25.967 --> 00:04:28.466
across the membrane of the endoplasmic reticulum

00:04:28.467 --> 00:04:31.266
that ends up eventually in the nuclear compartment

00:04:31.267 --> 00:04:33.666
where it turns up a vast gene expression program

00:04:33.667 --> 00:04:39.632
that makes, basically, more endoplasmic reticulum folding capacity, secretory capacity,

00:04:39.633 --> 00:04:44.366
capacity to degrade proteins that cannot be folded properly and so on and so forth.

00:04:44.367 --> 00:04:53.966
So it brings cells back into a homeostatic state that allows the load of secretory proteins

00:04:53.967 --> 00:04:58.832
to be balanced with the machinery available to carry out their task.

00:04:58.833 --> 00:05:06.332
To figure out how this pathway works two very adventurous graduate students in my lab,

00:05:06.333 --> 00:05:13.966
Jeff Cox and Carolyn Shamu, started a project in which we tried to identify

00:05:13.967 --> 00:05:17.266
these components genetically in the yeast system.

00:05:17.267 --> 00:05:21.766
And basically, what they did is they built a reporter system based on an observation

00:05:21.767 --> 00:05:25.832
made by Kazutoshi Mori in Mary-Jane Gething and Joe Sambrook's lab

00:05:25.833 --> 00:05:30.866
that there is a small element in the promoters of the target genes of the response

00:05:30.867 --> 00:05:35.166
that can be transplanted, can be put in front of a reporter gene,

00:05:35.167 --> 00:05:41.566
and then we can isolate mutants in the cell where when we induce unfolded proteins in the ER

00:05:41.567 --> 00:05:48.199
that no longer induce the Unfolded Protein Response by this induction of this reporter gene.

00:05:48.200 --> 00:05:52.599
We can then take these mutants, we can clone the genes that have been mutated

00:05:52.600 --> 00:05:57.399
by complementation and figure out what they do in the pathway.

00:05:57.400 --> 00:06:02.299
And the nice thing is that the first gene, which we isolated this way, turns out

00:06:02.300 --> 00:06:06.066
to be IRE1 and it encodes a transmembrane kinase.

00:06:06.067 --> 00:06:08.599
So by virtue of it being a transmembrane kinase,

00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:13.999
it already told us that this may be the signal transduction device

00:06:14.000 --> 00:06:18.166
sitting in the ER membrane, figuring out in one end what's going on there,

00:06:18.167 --> 00:06:20.932
and transmitting that information across the bilayer.

00:06:20.933 --> 00:06:25.399
Very nicely, the second gene we isolated turns out to be HAC1,

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:30.032
and HAC1 encodes a transcription factor that binds to all these promoter elements.

00:06:30.033 --> 00:06:34.399
So we then have the transmembrane kinase, we have a transcription factor,

00:06:34.400 --> 00:06:36.499
and of course the way we are thinking about that

00:06:36.500 --> 00:06:39.732
is very much in analogy to other transmembrane kinases,

00:06:39.733 --> 00:06:43.399
like growth factor receptors in the plasma membrane of mammalian cells,

00:06:43.400 --> 00:06:46.132
that this thing is activated and functions

00:06:46.133 --> 00:06:49.232
by a process of oligomerization in the plane of the membrane

00:06:49.233 --> 00:06:55.066
where as unfolded proteins accumulate, these kinase molecules come together,

00:06:55.067 --> 00:07:00.399
they start bringing the kinases together on the other side of the membrane

00:07:00.400 --> 00:07:04.666
where they are now juxtaposed so they can trans-autophosphorylate each other

00:07:04.667 --> 00:07:07.232
and that somehow leads to phosphorylation cascade

00:07:07.233 --> 00:07:09.966
downstream that activates the transcription factor.

00:07:09.967 --> 00:07:14.699
But it turns out that nothing could be further from the truth.

00:07:14.700 --> 00:07:20.299
This pathway is wired in a completely different way and a completely unexpected way.

00:07:20.300 --> 00:07:25.666
And that was discovered, pretty much, by a series of control experiments that Jeff carried out.

00:07:25.667 --> 00:07:30.566
And he was the first one, very simply, very naively,

00:07:30.567 --> 00:07:33.732
just started looking for the transcription factor in cells

00:07:33.733 --> 00:07:37.699
that are either induced for the response or that are not.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:41.332
And as you can see here, the transcription factor is only present

00:07:41.333 --> 00:07:45.866
in cells when the response is induced.

00:07:45.867 --> 00:07:49.166
So that tells us two things right then and there, right?

00:07:49.167 --> 00:07:51.166
It's either degraded when its not needed

00:07:51.167 --> 00:07:56.032
or it is only synthesized when the response is induced.

00:07:56.033 --> 00:08:00.499
And to distinguish between these two possibilities what Jeff did is

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:04.399
he just did a simple Northern blot analysis by which he asked,

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:07.132
Does the messenger RNA encoding this transcription factor

00:08:07.133 --> 00:08:11.299
change in its abundance when we induce unfolded proteins?

00:08:11.300 --> 00:08:16.932
As you see here, the messenger RNA doesn't really change much in abundance.

00:08:16.933 --> 00:08:23.699
But what you see, what Jeff discovered here, is that we now have a band of a different size

00:08:23.700 --> 00:08:29.632
that was completely unexpected.  So, the simple Northern blot then led to the discovery

00:08:29.633 --> 00:08:33.799
that there is something happening to the messenger RNA and to make a long story short

00:08:33.800 --> 00:08:37.066
it turns out that this messenger RNA becomes spliced.

00:08:37.067 --> 00:08:44.832
An intron is being removed when the Unfolded Protein Response is induced.

00:08:44.833 --> 00:08:47.832
So the idea then is, that the messenger RNA encoding

00:08:47.833 --> 00:08:52.232
the transcription factor is initially encoded with this intron

00:08:52.233 --> 00:08:57.732
and as the unfolded proteins accumulate, this intron is being removed,

00:08:57.733 --> 00:09:02.099
producing the spliced messenger RNA which is then being translated

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.932
to produce the transcription factor that turns up the response.

00:09:06.933 --> 00:09:12.532
Now this is highly unusual.  Normally, when cells decide to change their transcription

00:09:12.533 --> 00:09:15.966
to their splicing program through some alternative splicing,

00:09:15.967 --> 00:09:20.799
they make reasonably irreversible developmental decisions

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:26.532
so that they last a long time. They enter different cell fate with these decisions.

00:09:26.533 --> 00:09:27.732
But this is bona fide signaling.

00:09:27.733 --> 00:09:31.966
What's happening here is...strictly dependent on

00:09:31.967 --> 00:09:34.366
the conditions inside the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:09:34.367 --> 00:09:39.132
The signaling is on and off depending on whether you have unfolded proteins or not.

00:09:39.133 --> 00:09:42.532
What's even more surprising is that this splicing reaction

00:09:42.533 --> 00:09:45.699
followed none of the rules of normal messenger RNA splicing.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:52.199
It's completely independent of the spliceosome. It's happening in the cytosol and

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:56.232
it's carried out by two enzymes and two enzymes only.

00:09:56.233 --> 00:09:58.932
The first one turns out to be Ire1.

00:09:58.933 --> 00:10:05.932
Our transmembrane kinase when activated, becomes a bifunctional protein that is not only a kinase,

00:10:05.933 --> 00:10:11.066
but also a site specific endoribonuclease that cleaves the messenger RNA

00:10:11.067 --> 00:10:15.766
encoding the transcription factor precisely at both splice junctions

00:10:15.767 --> 00:10:20.399
and to the best of our knowledge, this is the only messenger RNA in the cell that it touches.

00:10:20.400 --> 00:10:23.532
And then, along comes an enzyme called tRNA ligase

00:10:23.533 --> 00:10:27.366
which was previously only known for its role in tRNA splicing

00:10:27.367 --> 00:10:32.132
and we had isolated a mutation in that, another graduate student, Carmela Sidrauski,

00:10:32.133 --> 00:10:35.866
and we couldn't make any sense out of that until Jeff had discovered

00:10:35.867 --> 00:10:40.332
that there's this RNA processing step in the signaling pathway.

00:10:40.333 --> 00:10:46.799
So the Unfolded Protein Response then transmits the signal by this unconventional,

00:10:46.800 --> 00:10:51.099
completely unprecedented splicing pathway

00:10:51.100 --> 00:10:56.399
and there's no phosphorylation cascade anywhere in sight here.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:11:01.466
Let me just show you this, too.  We can make this messenger RNA in vitro,

00:11:01.467 --> 00:11:04.732
we can incubate it with recombinantly produced Ire1

00:11:04.733 --> 00:11:07.432
and you see that the messenger RNA gets cleaved,

00:11:07.433 --> 00:11:13.632
producing the intron, 5' exon, 3' exon and then as we add also recombinantly produced,

00:11:13.633 --> 00:11:18.132
purified tRNA ligase to this reaction the exons go away,

00:11:18.133 --> 00:11:22.866
get ligated to form the product and the intron stays put.

00:11:22.867 --> 00:11:26.066
So we can reconstitute this whole pathway

00:11:26.067 --> 00:11:32.366
from two purified components with quite nice efficiency.

00:11:32.367 --> 00:11:39.366
To make a long story short, this discovery then led to many other labs and it turned out that

00:11:39.367 --> 00:11:43.466
pretty much everything...the cellular features that we've learned from

00:11:43.467 --> 00:11:51.799
the simple yeast system hold true for mammalian and metazoan cells.

00:11:51.800 --> 00:11:58.366
So this IRE1 exists in metazoan cells, it is involved in the splicing of a messenger RNA

00:11:58.367 --> 00:12:02.632
encoding a transcription factor, XBP1, XPB1 here.

00:12:02.633 --> 00:12:08.032
Things are more complicated, higher evolved eukaryotic cells have

00:12:08.033 --> 00:12:10.866
added more bells and whistles to the pathway.

00:12:10.867 --> 00:12:16.066
So we have three parallel pathways here that transmits information from the ER lumen

00:12:16.067 --> 00:12:19.932
to the cytosol each leading to the activation of a transcription factor.

00:12:19.933 --> 00:12:24.232
We have Ire1 working by this non-conventional mRNA splicing.

00:12:24.233 --> 00:12:27.432
We have another transmembrane kinase here, PERK

00:12:27.433 --> 00:12:32.232
that makes another transcription factor by a mechanism of translational control.

00:12:32.233 --> 00:12:34.699
And finally, we have this exciting protein here

00:12:34.700 --> 00:12:39.332
that sits in the ER membrane, ATF6, and then gets released,

00:12:39.333 --> 00:12:43.199
a fragment of it gets released to become an active transcription factor

00:12:43.200 --> 00:12:47.499
that moves into the nucleus and turns up the target genes only when

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:51.799
unfolded proteins are accumulating in the endoplasmic reticulum.

00:12:51.800 --> 00:12:54.832
And the idea of the whole thing is the same as I told you before.

00:12:54.833 --> 00:12:59.766
It is to establish, re-establish homeostasis so protein folding

00:12:59.767 --> 00:13:03.132
in the endoplasmic reticulum can occur with fidelity.

00:13:03.133 --> 00:13:07.332
So we have these three pathways here and they establish homeostasis.

00:13:07.333 --> 00:13:15.466
But I also told you that there is a danger that if cells cannot achieve homeostasis

00:13:15.467 --> 00:13:20.632
that they may make mistakes in protein folding and therefore turn into rogue cells

00:13:20.633 --> 00:13:24.366
that endanger an organism. So there's a safety valve built in

00:13:24.367 --> 00:13:29.266
that if this balance cannot be achieved again that cells, rather than

00:13:29.267 --> 00:13:36.099
putting these misfolded proteins, and misfolded signaling machines on the cell surface

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:39.366
go down a pathway of apoptosis.

00:13:39.367 --> 00:13:43.366
So rather than becoming rogue cells and endangering the organism,

00:13:43.367 --> 00:13:45.599
they remove themselves by committing suicide.

00:13:45.600 --> 00:13:50.532
And it's this point that the Unfolded Protein Response makes

00:13:50.533 --> 00:13:56.666
life death decisions for the cell that puts this pathway in the midst of many different human diseases.

00:13:56.667 --> 00:14:06.799
Some cancer cells are kept alive because the response gives them a growth advantage.

00:14:06.800 --> 00:14:13.899
In diabetes, the beta cells in the pancreas may die through this apoptotic route here

00:14:13.900 --> 00:14:19.732
by being over-committed to produce ever increasing amounts of insulin.

00:14:19.733 --> 00:14:24.432
And there are neurodegenerative diseases where protein misfolding causes apoptosis

00:14:24.433 --> 00:14:26.866
by turning up this response.

00:14:26.867 --> 00:14:35.599
So, basically then, our very pioneering work of these very adventurous graduate students

00:14:35.600 --> 00:14:41.899
has led us to understand a mechanism that we now, we and many other labs

00:14:41.900 --> 00:14:50.599
have tried to utilize in therapeutic approaches to see if we can have

00:14:50.600 --> 00:14:58.332
some ability of bettering mankind via interfering and modulating these pathways

00:14:58.333 --> 00:15:00.766
and therefore affecting the outcome of human disease.

00:15:00.767 --> 00:15:09.199
And I should close by giving credit to these really wonderful, adventurous pioneers:

00:15:09.200 --> 00:15:16.399
Jeff Cox, who discovered, together with Carolyn Shamu, Ire1 and the Hac1 transcription factor

00:15:16.400 --> 00:15:24.232
and Carmela Sidrauski, who discovered that Ire1 is a nuclease in the splicing reaction and

00:15:24.233 --> 01:00:00.000
put the tRNA ligase on the map.

